{"authored" [{"question" "q-325177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The polar equation of the circle that is centered at the origin and that passes through the point $A(6,2)$ is$\\,\\,r=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039331/state.json" "file_sha256" "d305f030b0d51e51ac65f710da544a0ebe0db7c6274cd50d05a0a4e2b40bdf14" "basis" 1357 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039331/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-325177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A circle of radius $k$ centered at the origin has the polar equation $r=k.$\n\nWe can compute the radius $k$ of our circle using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is $r=2\\sqrt{10}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039331/state.json" "file_sha256" "d305f030b0d51e51ac65f710da544a0ebe0db7c6274cd50d05a0a4e2b40bdf14" "basis" 1357 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039331/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-325177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039331/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "430ff7c720a1034b1607f771961275fd4768d791245511665b132c3e38f030c4" "basis" 1357 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039331/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-325177" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039331/state.json" "file_sha256" "d305f030b0d51e51ac65f710da544a0ebe0db7c6274cd50d05a0a4e2b40bdf14" "basis" 1357 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039331/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-251446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "**From left to right**, the points of intersection of the parabola ${x}^{2}=4(y+\\frac{1}{2})$ with the $x$-axis are $A$ and $B.$ Find the **exact** coordinates of $A$ and $B.$\n\n$A(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\,$ and $\\,B(${{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-251446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "When the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4(0+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the intersection points, from left to right, are $(-\\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(\\sqrt{2},0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-251446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aaf8d8eeff383da3408dc13cdfcd4224bc9407ed5e2c592fb4d9e9aa755cbaf7"} {"question" "q-251446" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-251446" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-251446" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-251446" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-251446" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-251446" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-251446" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-251446" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-341918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(9,\\,4)$ for the curve $\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-341918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2}-{y}^{1/2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(9,\\,4)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(9,\\,4)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{x}})|}_{(9,\\,4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4}}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-341918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aaf8d8eeff383da3408dc13cdfcd4224bc9407ed5e2c592fb4d9e9aa755cbaf7"} {"question" "q-341918" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-341918" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-120635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $3-4\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x<{180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-120635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-4\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\text{arctan}(\\frac{2}{3})=(33.690\\,0…)^{∘}≈{33.690}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${33.690}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={33.690}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add and subtract integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=-{180}^{∘}+{33.690}^{∘}=-{146.310}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-{146.310}^{∘},\\,{33.690}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n(-146.310)\\cdot 33.690≈-4\\,929\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-120635" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aaf8d8eeff383da3408dc13cdfcd4224bc9407ed5e2c592fb4d9e9aa755cbaf7"} {"question" "q-120635" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-120635" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4\\,929"] ["math" "-6\\,346"] ["math" "-7\\,235"] ["math" "-7\\,897"] ["math" "-8\\,124"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-120635" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4\\,929"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-325177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The polar equation of the circle that is centered at the origin and that passes through the point $A(6,2)$ is$\\,\\,r=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-325177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A circle of radius $k$ centered at the origin has the polar equation $r=k.$\n\nWe can compute the radius $k$ of our circle using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is $r=2\\sqrt{10}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-325177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aaf8d8eeff383da3408dc13cdfcd4224bc9407ed5e2c592fb4d9e9aa755cbaf7"} {"question" "q-325177" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-325177" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{10}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-281343" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a complex root $x=2+5\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $1,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-281343" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=2+5\\text{i}$ is a root, we know that its complex conjugate $x=2-5\\text{i}$ is also a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(2+5\\text{i}))(x-(2-5\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(2+5\\text{i})-x(2-5\\text{i})+(2+5\\text{i})(2-5\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x-\\cancel{5x\\text{i}}-2x+\\cancel{5x\\text{i}}+4-25{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+25 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+29 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-281343" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "aaf8d8eeff383da3408dc13cdfcd4224bc9407ed5e2c592fb4d9e9aa755cbaf7"} {"question" "q-281343" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"} {"question" "q-281343" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x^2-4x+29"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040402/state.json" "file_sha256" "b746a449e39ba607ee479dee3a99cdb87ec7e2441c7c0ca6d681edf86c899d29"}] "usage" {"q-325177" []} "basis" 1454 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [17592186302448 17592186302449] "immutable_fields" [[17592186302447 :answer-field/correct] [17592186302447 :answer-field/id] [17592186302447 :answer-field/key] [17592186302447 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
